thread:
Casey: Noticed a couple of subtle but annoying things in the compact playground on narrow widths during streaming: token counter width shifts, stop/regenerate group gets shoved off-screen by quick-actions, and choosing a prompt from the library sometimes restarts the stream. Attached a small screen capture.
Morgan: link: https://files.internal/s/compact-stream-glitch.mov — can repro on Android emulation.
Lina: I think we can fix layout jump two ways: (A) reserve width for token count using a monospace fallback + min-width, or (B) render a fixed-width placeholder (e.g., 00000) and overlay the live number. (B) is low-risk.
Devon: Backend emits chunks ~40-90ms. If we update the DOM on every chunk we cause layout thrash. Suggest client-side throttle via rAF batch or a 120ms debounce. rAF batching + last-value commit seems snappy and cheap.
Priya: Accessibility: prefer aria-live="polite" for streaming so screen readers don't interrupt, but we should give a manual "Announce latest" control which uses aria-live="assertive" on demand. Also ensure the floating control has an accessible name: aria-label="Stop generation" (visible label can be "Stop").
Casey: UX question: floating stop overlay vs collapsing quick-actions. On tiny devices discoverability matters. Floating overlay with 44px touch target seems best, but it mustn't cover attachments or file previews. Maybe pin it to the top-right of the message frame with safe insets.
Lina: Proposed CSS for token placeholder and stop float:
```css
.playground .token-badge { 
  font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace; 
  min-width: 4.5ch; /* reserve width for up to 9999 */ 
  text-align: right; 
}
.playground .stop-float { 
  width: 44px; height: 44px; 
  position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 12px; 
  border-radius: 22px; box-shadow: 0 6px 18px rgba(0,0,0,0.12); 
}
```
Devon: rAF batch + timeout fallback example (pseudo):
```js
let pending = null;
function onChunk(count) {
  lastCount = count;
  if (!pending) {
    pending = requestAnimationFrame(() => {
      renderToken(lastCount);
      pending = null;
    });
    // ensure update if rAF delays too long
    setTimeout(() => { if (pending) { cancelAnimationFrame(pending); renderToken(lastCount); pending = null } }, 200);
  }
}
```
Priya: Keyboard + shortcuts: allow Esc to stop stream and also expose a keyboard affordance (Shift+Cmd+K) to toggle the floating controls visibility for users who prefer less visual clutter. Focus trap rules: when floating stop receives focus it should not trap focus inside the message area. Visible focus ring required.
Morgan: Copy nit: change tooltip from "Stop" to "Stop generation" for screen readers but keep visible tooltip short. For regenerate, visible: "Regenerate", aria-label: "Regenerate response". Also token tooltip should read numeric usage: "Tokens: 340 of 1024 (33%)".
Lina: Prompt library insertion issue: current flow does a full composer hydration which tears down the streaming session. Instead implement an insertText API that updates the textarea value and caret, keeping the websocket session open. If we must rehydrate, snapshot session id and resume without a fresh generation request.
Devon: I'll implement the rAF batch and the insertText minimal DOM patch. Backend exposes a resume token; we'll attempt to reuse it rather than re-request. I'll add tests around stream continuity.
Casey: design-bot preview? want to validate visual density at 320px and 360px widths, plus high-contrast mode.
design-bot: Preview: https://playground.internal/preview/compact-ux-218765 (includes mobile emulation and a11y overlays). Visual diff comments linked on PR.
Priya: I'll draft a short a11y acceptance checklist: keyboard stop/regenerate focus order, aria-live usage, announce token thresholds (75% and 90%) with explicit counts, visible focus ring, 44px touch targets, and screen-reader text for tooltips.
Morgan: Plan: Lina to deliver mock for floating stop + token placeholder; Devon to land rAF batch + insertText; Priya to own a11y checklist; I'll review preview and merge if checks pass. Pair sessions: Lina+Priya on visual contrast at 3pm, Devon+Morgan on stream continuity at 4pm.
Casey: Thanks — I'll file the follow-ups on the design board and tag beta users for testing once merged.
